  urldate = {2025-11-28},
  abstract = {The Fock space is an algebraic construction used in quantum mechanics to construct the quantum states space of a variable or unknown number of identical particles from a single particle Hilbert space H. It is named after V. A. Fock who first introduced it in his 1932 paper "Konfigurationsraum und zweite Quantelung" ("Configuration space and second quantization"). Informally, a Fock space is the sum of a set of Hilbert spaces representing zero particle states, one particle states, two particle states, and so on. If the identical particles are bosons, the n-particle states are vectors in a symmetrized tensor product of n single-particle Hilbert spaces H. If the identical particles are fermions, the n-particle states are vectors in an antisymmetrized tensor product of n single-particle Hilbert spaces H (see symmetric algebra and exterior algebra respectively). A general state in Fock space is a linear combination of n-particle states, one for each n. Technically, the Fock space is (the Hilbert space completion of) the direct sum of the symmetric or antisymmetric tensors in the tensor powers of a single-particle Hilbert space H,                                   F                        {$\nu$}                             (         H         )         =                                                                 {$\bigoplus$}                                    n                   =                   0                                                     {$\infty$}                                                                S                                    {$\nu$}                                                                H                                    {$\otimes$}                   n                                                          \textasciimacron{}                                       .                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle F\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash nu \textbraceright (H)=\textbraceleft\textbackslash overline \textbraceleft\textbackslash bigoplus \_\textbraceleft n=0\textbraceright\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash infty \textbraceright S\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash nu \textbraceright H\textasciicircum\textbraceleft\textbackslash otimes n\textbraceright\textbraceright\textbraceright\textasciitilde.\textbraceright{}    Here                                    S                        {$\nu$}                                     \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle S\_\textbraceleft\textbackslash nu \textbraceright\textbraceright{}     is the operator that symmetrizes or antisymmetrizes a tensor, depending on whether the Hilbert space describes particles obeying bosonic                         (         {$\nu$}         =         +         )                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle (\textbackslash nu =+)\textbraceright{}     or fermionic                         (         {$\nu$}         =         -         )                 \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle (\textbackslash nu =-)\textbraceright{}     statistics, and the overline represents the completion of the space. The bosonic (or fermionic) Fock space can alternatively be constructed as (the Hilbert space completion of) the symmetric tensors                                    F                        +                             (         H         )         =                                                                 S                                    {$\ast$}                                               H                          \textasciimacron{}                                     \textbraceleft\textbackslash displaystyle F\_\textbraceleft +\textbraceright (H)=\textbraceleft\textbackslash overline \textbraceleft S\textasciicircum\textbraceleft *\textbraceright H\textbraceright\textbraceright\textbraceright{}     (or alternating tensors                                    F                        -                             (         H         )         =                                                                                    {$\bigwedge$}                                                     {$\ast$}                                               H                          \textasciimacron{}                                     \textbraceleft\textbackslash textstyle F\_\textbraceleft -\textbraceright (H)=\textbraceleft\textbackslash overline \textbraceleft\textbraceleft\textbackslash bigwedge \textbraceright\textasciicircum\textbraceleft *\textbraceright H\textbraceright\textbraceright\textbraceright{}    ). For every basis of H, there is a natural basis of the Fock space, the Fock states.},
